<< /A 40 0 R /Alt (Timeline graphic highlights major events from June to August 2024 related to quota protests, including court rulings, student demonstrations, blockades, violent clashes, curfew, internet blackout, mass casualties, and a political resignation.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 30 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 41 0 R /Alt (Flowchart detailing the enrollment, data collection, and data curation stages of a mental health workshop study for Bangladeshi individuals, including steps for consent, workshop participation, survey methodology, and participant data filtering.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Two side-by-side choropleth maps of Bangladesh compare administrative regions. Panel a uses shades of blue to indicate the number of study participants, with the darkest regions having the highest counts. Panel b uses shades of green to depict population density per square kilometer, with darker regions representing higher population density. Each map includes a gradient legend showing the respective data ranges.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Stacked bar chart showing distribution of sociodemographic variables by divisions in Bangladesh, with categories for age groups, gender, and religion. Dhaka and Chattogram dominate across most variables, with smaller segments for other divisions and abroad.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Forest plot graphic showing prevalence ratios and confidence intervals for high PCL score \(greater than or equal to 31\) by variable levels: generation, major status, gender, religion, with a red dashed vertical reference line at prevalence ratio one.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (Two grouped bar charts compare different demographic categories across four variable groups: age, gender, religion, and division. The top chart shows PAF percentages, with Millennials and NonMuslims having the highest positive values and GenX_Boomer and Men showing negative values. The lower chart shows ARR percentages, where NonMuslims and Millennials again have the highest positive percentages, and GenX_Boomer and Men are negative. Legends clarify color associations for each variable group.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (Grouped horizontal bar chart showing the mean of PCL clusters B, C, D, and E by age, gender, religion, and region. Sylhet and Buddhism show the highest mean values in Cluster E compared to other groups.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Radar chart illustrating the normalized mean values of PCL clusters with five axes labeled CLUSTER-B \(0.22\), CLUSTER-C \(0.08\), CLUSTER-D \(0.19\), CLUSTER-E \(0.15\), and an unlabeled fifth axis. CLUSTER-B has the highest value and CLUSTER-C the lowest.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
